  "response": "I’d request changes and withhold approval if `tomorrowMs` promises “the same local time tomorrow.” Adding 24 hours measures elapsed time, not a calendar day.\n\nThe finding’s evidence is:\n\n> “+ 86400000 (day) @L52”  \n> “lines: 52”  \n> “use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math”\n\nIt fails around the two local DST transition dates each year:\n\n- The spring-forward date, when the day is 23 hours.\n- The fall-back date, when the day is 25 hours.\n\nThus the result can be an hour late or early in local wall-clock time. The exact dates depend on the year and timezone—for Pacific/Auckland in 2026, they are April 5 and September 27. Use calendar-aware “add one day” logic with an explicit timezone. If the helper intentionally means “exactly 24 elapsed hours later,” it should be renamed and documented accordingly.",
